      "BooleanSuppliers wired to commands Buttons, sensor predicates, custom expressions — anything that evaluates to a boolean. Bindings are scoped (global / opmode / command), so they clean themselves up when the scope exits. One physical thing each An arm, a flywheel, the drivetrain. The type is Mechanism , a class you extend. Hardware lives in private fields, configuration in the constructor. Named actions, mostly holds Command . Most of ours are holds — they keep the motor at a setpoint until something else takes over. The big picture Holds: what our commands actually are Our mechanism commands — arm.scoring() flywheel.spinUp() robot.stow() and stays there , fighting gravity; let go, and the arm's default command takes back over. Put a hold inside Command.sequence(...) and the sequence sticks on it forever (hold) so you can catch this: if a stuck routine is sitting on a command on the dashboard or in the log, that's the bug. The fix is to give the hold a finish line at the call site: arm.scoring().until(arm::isAtTarget) Chaining: how routines get built Routines — especially autos — are built by chaining . Chaining is as far as most routines ever need to go, and it takes just three tools, learned in this order: Command.sequence(a, b, c) DriveToPose leg, for example) can sit in a sequence as-is. .until(mech::isAtTarget) Command.race(step, hold) Plus one seatbelt: .withTimeout(Seconds.of(...)) on any step that waits on a sensor condition, so an auto never burns the whole period stuck at a setpoint it can't quite reach. is a robot-level preset the template defines — it drives the arm to its stowed angle, which is why the finish line checks robot.arm::isAtTarget . Your own holds will usually live on a mechanism, like arm.stowed() Decorators decorator is a method on the interface that returns a wrapped command with some behavior added. The ones below cover the cases that come up daily. .named(...) is required: the WPILib compiler plugin makes an unnamed command a build error, so every builder chain ends in .whenCanceled(...) Time (e.g. Seconds.of(...) double , in keeping with v3's units-everywhere policy. And note what is not ...AndWait variants on mechanisms — waiting is always spelled .until(...) at the call site. The advanced dialects (you can skip these) Commands V3 has two more ways to write routines. You don't need either one for this workshop — chaining covers everything we build — but you should know they exist so the template code doesn't surprise you. coroutine.await(command); DriveStowDriveOpMode.java machine.when(...) StateMachineTeleop.java Implementation sequence The workshop builds up a command-based project in this order. Each step assumes the previous one is in place. Mechanisms setDefaultCommand Commands — hold factories on each mechanism, named with a suffix. Triggers — controller bindings + the scoping rules (global / opmode / command). PID control — closed-loop requests for the holds to re-send. Motion Magic — profiled-position requests with acceleration and cruise-velocity bounds. Auto routines — chained sequences of drive legs and holds, exactly the pattern above. Ground truth for this lesson Our team's reference implementation, with the hold and chaining rules written up in its onboarding guide: Commands V3 — the staged builder, the compile-time naming enforcement, and the StateMachine Java 25 and deploys to SystemCore . The stack is the WPILib 2027 alpha 2027.0.0-alpha-6 , Phoenix 6 26.50.0-alpha-1 ), so the exact APIs are still moving between alpha builds. This page was last verified against alpha-6 in July 2026. Command-Based Framework The Command-Based Framework Command-based programming organizes robot code into three pieces — Triggers (when), Mechanisms (what hardware), and Commands (the actions to run on that hardware). The scheduler is the loop that ties them together: it watches Triggers, schedules Commands, and tracks which command owns which Mechanism so two commands never fight for the same motor. Commands V3 gives each piece a concrete type: Mechanism for hardware, Command for the actions, and Scheduler for the loop. On this team, most commands are holds — they keep re-sending a setpoint so the motor stays actively commanded — and routines are built by chaining commands together. The top-level wiring: a Robot class owns the mechanisms, and each mode — driver teleop, an autonomous routine, a calibration task — is its own OpMode class. You Triggers schedule Commands. Commands operate on Mechanisms. The Scheduler enforces who-owns-what so nothing collides. concept WHEN WHAT HOW simple var(--fg) -0.01em var(--fg-mute) java A hold — runRepeatedly re-sends the closed-loop request // From the 2027-Template scoring (hold) alert-warning THE ONE RULE A hold never finishes, so nothing may ever wait on a hold The reference routine — drive, stow, drive (from the 2027-Template) stow until stowed drive holding stow Drive Stow Drive (Chained) DriveStowDriveChainedOpMode.java — the full working example The decorators you scoring until there scoring capped scoring safeCancel eStop alert-info NOTE · DECORATOR RULES A few rules worth knowing OPTIONAL · COROUTINES fork / await / waitUntil _blank noopener noreferrer OPTIONAL · STATE MACHINE 2027-Template ONBOARDING.md — Holds never finish WPILib Command-Based Programming Guide NOTE · API STATUS This is the WPILib 2027 alpha Knowledge Check What are the three pieces of the command-based framework, and which one owns hardware? Inputs, Logic, Outputs — Outputs owns hardware Triggers, Mechanisms, Commands — Mechanisms owns hardware Sensors, Subsystems, Scheduler — Subsystems owns hardware Buttons, Routines, Motors — Motors owns hardware Triggers say when to run something, Mechanisms own the hardware (motors, sensors, configuration), and Commands are the actions that get scheduled. Which decorator is enforced at compile time by the WPILib compiler plugin? .until(BooleanSupplier) .named(String) .whenCanceled(Runnable) .withPriority(int) .named(...) is enforced — every command-builder chain has to end with a name call or the project won s also why every hold carries a \\ suffix: the name is what you You put arm.scoring() — a hold — directly into Command.sequence(driveLeg, arm.scoring(), nextLeg). What happens when the auto runs? The arm reaches the scoring angle and the sequence moves on The sequence sticks on the hold forever — nextLeg never starts The scheduler throws an exception at schedule time The hold is skipped because sequences ignore non-finishing commands THE ONE RULE: a hold never finishes, so nothing may ever wait on a hold. The sequence waits on scoring (hold) forever. On the dashboard you\\ What Call arm.scoringAndWait() instead Give the hold a finish line at the call site: arm.scoring().until(arm::isAtTarget) Wrap the hold in Command.parallel so the sequence doesn Lower the hold Waiting is always spelled at the call site with .until(...) — there are no methods on mechanisms. Add .withTimeout(...) as a seatbelt if the target might be unreachable. In Command.race(new DriveToPose(robot.drivetrain, pose2), robot.stow()), which member decides when the race ends? Whichever finishes first — it could be either DriveToPose — the hold never finishes, so the drive leg is always what decides, and the race cancels the hold robot.stow() — holds win races because they run at higher priority Neither — a race needs an explicit .until(...) to end A race ends when its first member finishes and cancels the rest. Since a hold never finishes, the self-finishing step is always the decider — that\\ do this step WHILE holding that pose.",
