    content:
      'Turning on logging DataLogManager is part of WPILib, so there\'s nothing to install. Start it in Robot \'s constructor before anything else, and add the Driver-Station data feed: Simulation: .wpilog ./logs in your project. On SystemCore: a USB drive if one is plugged in, otherwise /home/systemcore/logs Phoenix 6 devices .hoot file (high-rate signal data) that you can open in Tuner X or AdvantageScope, so you get that extra detail for free. What you get automatically The moment DataLogManager.start() runs, these are captured with no further code: Every NetworkTables value change , including everything your telemetry publishes (more below) Console output : anything printed to stdout/stderr Driver-Station data startDataLog ): alliance, mode, match time, and full joystick axes/buttons Phoenix 6 signals in the file: motor positions, velocities, currents, temperatures Logging your own values publish it to NetworkTables SmartDashboard.putNumber("Arm/Position", pos) Pose2d ChassisVelocities struct publisher so AdvantageScope can render them natively. The template\'s Telemetry drivetrain.registerTelemetry(telemetry::telemeterize) ). Because it\'s on NetworkTables, too. pose = table.getStructTopic("Pose", Pose2d.struct).publish(); private final StructPublisher v3 mechanisms don\'t have a periodic() method, so "publish my state every loop" needs a different home. The simple pattern is a runRepeatedly(...) default command that publishes each tick whenever nothing else is using the mechanism: You can also publish from inside a command body, right next to the setpoint that produced the value — handy for logging target-vs-actual during a move. Use hierarchical keys "Arm/Position" ) so the log stays organized. Prefer struct types rather than three separate numbers. Avoid high-frequency strings . They\'re expensive; log numbers and booleans. Don\'t over-publish . Too much NT traffic can affect loop timing. Workshop Code Implementation Robot.java — starting DataLogManager records all of it. AdvantageScope AdvantageScope is the natural viewer for the logs you\'re producing. It reads files.) 📡 Real-Time Data Viewing Everything you publish to NetworkTables is visible live. Open AdvantageScope on your driver station Select "Connect to Robot" from the menu Enter your team number or robot address Browse the NetworkTables tree — your keys land under their table (e.g. Drivetrain/Pose Add graphs, the 3D field view, or swerve module visualizations 📥 Post-Match Log Analysis Pull files off the USB drive or download them from the robot with AdvantageScope. Open Preferences and set the robot address / log folder Click "File" > "Download Logs..." Select the logs to download (newest at top) Open the downloaded file Add line graphs or use the 3D field view for analysis Your published values land under whatever table you chose, and the auto-captured data sits alongside it: Drivetrain/Velocity , …: your telemetry struct publishers SmartDashboard/... SmartDashboard.put* Overlay multiple signals: compare target vs actual on the same graph Video sync: line log data up with match video Save layouts: reusable dashboards for quick analysis Drag struct types in: onto the 2D/3D field view directly Logging Best Practices Log sensor inputs, motor outputs, and target setpoints together Use hierarchical keys ( Subsystem/Parameter Prefer struct types ( SwerveModuleVelocity[] ) over flattened number arrays Start DataLogManager first thing in Robot\'s constructor Download logs after every match and review between matches Log high-frequency strings (use numbers or booleans) Publish so much that NetworkTables traffic hurts loop timing Forget a USB drive on the robot if you want the log off the controller easily Ignore loop-overrun warnings from excessive publishing Additional Resources What\'s Next? With DataLogManager recording your pose, velocities, and module states, you\'re ready to implement autonomous navigation, with every PID setpoint, error, and motor output captured for tuning the moment something looks off. lucide-react Implementing Logging Setting Up DataLogManager We log with WPILib s constructor turn it on; after that, anything published to NetworkTables (WPILib Turn it on in Robot Robot.java java alert-info Where the logs go alert-tip Captured for free 🗺️ The drivetrain telemetry surface (struct publishers) Telemetry.java Drivetrain Pose Velocity TranslationSpeedMps 📊 Logging from a mechanism (there Arm.java Arm Position Arm:telemetry alert-warning Performance Considerations Hemlock5712/Workshop-Code 2-Logging 🔧 Drivetrain telemetry Where the data lives in NetworkTables AdvantageScope Pro Tips AdvantageScope Documentation alert-success ✅ Do alert-danger ❌ Don&rsquo;t WPILib DataLogManager Documentation NetworkTables (publishing values) AdvantageScope - Log Visualization Phoenix 6 (.hoot signal logging) Knowledge Check How do you turn on logging in the 2027 template? Extend LoggedRobot and add the AdvantageKit vendordep Call DataLogManager.start() and DriverStation.startDataLog(...) in Robot Annotate every field with @Logged Enable it from the Driver Station settings DataLogManager is built into WPILib. Two lines in Robot What does DataLogManager capture automatically? Only values you pass to Logger.recordOutput(...) Every NetworkTables value change, console output, and (via startDataLog) Driver-Station + joystick data Only Phoenix 6 motor signals Nothing until you annotate fields with @Logged Once started, DataLogManager records all NetworkTables value changes and console output to a .wpilog; startDataLog adds the Driver-Station state and joystick data. Phoenix 6 devices also log signals to a separate .hoot file. How do you get a custom value (say, the arm Call Logger.recordOutput( , pos) Publish it to NetworkTables (SmartDashboard.putNumber or a NT publisher) — DataLogManager records NT changes Write it to a text file yourself It can DataLogManager logs whatever changes on NetworkTables, so you log a custom value by publishing it there — SmartDashboard.putNumber for a quick number, or a NetworkTables struct publisher for types like Pose2d. Why publish a Pose2d through a NetworkTables struct publisher instead of three separate doubles? Three doubles aren A struct publisher keeps it as one type-aware value, so AdvantageScope can drop it straight onto the 2D/3D field view It uses less battery Struct publishers are required for DataLogManager to run Pose2d (and ChassisVelocities, SwerveModuleVelocity[], etc.) are WPILib struct types. Publishing them through a StructPublisher keeps them as one coherent value that AdvantageScope reconstructs and renders natively — no manual axis wiring. A v3 Mechanism has no periodic() method. Where do you put per-loop telemetry publishing? Override periodic() — Mechanism still has it In a runRepeatedly(this::publishTelemetry).named(...) default command, or from inside command bodies Telemetry isn In Robot.robotPeriodic() for every mechanism Mechanism intentionally drops periodic(). Set a runRepeatedly(...) default command to publish each loop while the mechanism is idle, or publish from inside command bodies next to the setpoint. The drivetrain uses a CTRE-registered Telemetry callback instead. Up Next: Drive to Point',
