    title: "State Machines",
    description:
      "Optional advanced lesson: the Commands V3 StateMachine class — named states owning hold commands, declared transitions, entry/exit hooks, and any-state interrupts. Chaining covers everyday routines; reach for this when phases repeat, skip, or recover.",
    content:
      "StateMachine StateMachineTeleop.java in the 2027-Template This lesson teaches the official Commands-v3 org.wpilib.command3.StateMachine ), released in WPILib 2027 alpha-6 in May 2026. Everything on this page is checked against the published source. Alpha APIs can still shift before the 2027 kickoff release, but this is the real class, not a preview sketch. Anatomy of a StateMachine Command . Transitions are declared on the states themselves and are checked every scheduler tick while that state is active. arm.stowed() and friends are the same hold factories you built on the Mechanisms page — runRepeatedly re-sending a setpoint, named \"stowed (hold)\" waits on the hold; transitions watch conditions instead. 6-StateBased flywheel::isAtSpeed switchFromAny panic interrupt. Transitions come in two flavors. Use the right one for the kind of state you're leaving. while the state's command is running . Rising-edge guarded, so state.switchTo(state).when(...) never infinite loops — the condition has to go false and then true again to fire a second time. Does not fire for one-shot commands that never yield — the loop checking it never runs. Checked once, after the state's command finishes on its own. Use this for self-finishing state commands (like a one-shot fireOnce() whenComplete() on a hold-backed state will never fire — use .when(...) there instead. whenCompleteAnd(cond) when both apply. Each state can register any number of onEnter onExit callbacks. Useful when entering or leaving a state needs to kick off side-effects that aren't part of the state's main command: schedule a background animation, stiffen the drivetrain, log a marker, etc. Callbacks fire in the order they were added. Entry callbacks run immediately after the state's command is scheduled, so they can see it running. Exit callbacks run just before the command is canceled (on a transition) or just after it finishes (on a completion). switchFromAny(...) sm.switchFromAny(state1, state2).to(state3).when(...) sm.switchFromAny().toExitStateMachine().when(eStop) state.exitStateMachine().when(...) Set currentState to the initial state. The state's command is forked, then Each scheduler tick, every conditional transition is checked in declaration order. The first rising edge wins: without an extra yield If the command finishes on its own, completion transitions are checked once ( whenCompleteAnd whenComplete ). The first matching target becomes the next state. If no transition matches when the command finishes, the machine exits. Where this sits next to the other dialects Commands V3 gives you three ways to express a routine, and this team teaches them in this order: chaining Command.sequence + call-site .until race ) is the everyday tool and as far as most routines ever need to go; coroutines fork / await / waitUntil DriveStowDriveChainedOpMode.java DriveStowDriveOpMode.java StateMachineTeleop.java Commands V3 and the Java 25 and deploy to SystemCore . The stack is the WPILib 2027 alpha 2027.0.0-alpha-6 ) — the release where StateMachine first shipped. This page was last verified against alpha-6 in July 2026. State Machines State Machines with WPILib Commands V3 A state machine models a system as a set of discrete states, an active behavior per state, and transitions that move between them. WPILib This is an optional, advanced dialect. In everyday teleop, each button holds a superstructure preset (whileTrue) — hold A, the scoring hold runs; let go, the default command comes back. With a state machine, the robot is instead always in exactly one named state, and buttons/sensors move it between states. The machine cancels the old state t exist because no transition was declared for them. A state is a Command that runs while the machine is in it. Transitions are edge-triggered conditions that cancel the current state alert-tip OPTIONAL · ADVANCED You don _blank noopener noreferrer alert-info OFFICIAL · WPILIB 2027 ALPHA-6 StateMachine is a shipped WPILib API StateMachineTeleop.java — the team WPILib State Machine Design Doc StateMachine API Pull Request var(--fg) -0.01em var(--fg-mute) java Superstructure as a state machine (the StateMachineTeleop pattern) Superstructure re the mechanism (hold) get safe now Hemlock5712/Workshop-Code Two kinds of transitions concept WHEN · CONDITIONAL switchTo(...).when(cond) WHEN · COMPLETION switchTo(...).whenComplete() Mixing conditional and completion transitions Entry and exit hooks Lock the swerve wheels in X when leaving the drive-up state Cross-cutting transitions with switchFromAny LED priority machine: warning beats info beats idle StateMachine sm = new StateMachine( Lifecycle, in one breath NOTE · API STATUS This is the WPILib 2027 alpha Knowledge check A state runs a Command that completes immediately (a one-shot, no yield). Which kind of transition will move you to the next state? switchTo(next).when(cond) switchTo(next).whenComplete() Either — they Neither; one-shot states aren Conditional transitions (.when) are checked inside the loop that runs while the state s checked exactly once after the command finishes. What does sm.switchFromAny().to(safeState).when(eStop) do? Adds an eStop transition only to the initial state Schedules safeState as the new default command for every mechanism Adds the same transition to every state that exists in the machine at the time the call is made Replaces every existing transition with this one switchFromAny() with no arguments is shorthand for adding the transition to every state currently in the machine. States added after this call don ve added all the states you want them to apply to. Why is setInitialState() required, and how does WPILib enforce it? Runtime check only — you It auto-defaults to the first state added; you only need it to override setInitialState() is marked @PostConstructionInitializer, and the WPILib compiler plugin fails the build if you construct a StateMachine and never call it — the same build-time enforcement Commands V3 applies to .named() on command builders. As a backstop, a machine that reaches the scheduler without one throws IllegalStateException when it starts. A conditional transition fires while a state The next state The state The scheduler yields, then everything happens on the next tick onExit fires after the next state Transitions are synchronous within a single scheduler iteration. onExit runs first, then the current command is canceled, then the next state becomes current and its command is forked — all without an extra yield. This is intentional so a chain of fast transitions doesn What Nothing — StateMachine is implemented as a Command.sequence internally StateMachine supports transitions back to earlier phases and entry/exit hooks; Command.sequence only runs phases in order, top to bottom Command.sequence is faster because it doesn StateMachine can only have two states; Command.sequence is unlimited Command.sequence(...) is a linear pipeline — A then B then C. A StateMachine is a graph: any state can transition to any other state at any time, with onEnter/onExit hooks and switchFromAny interrupts. If your routine can repeat phases, skip phases, or recover by jumping back, you want a state machine, not a sequence.",
